        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The project was an effort to improve the operational performance of the first stage of the ultrafiltration/reverse osmosis water processor used in the Lunar-Mars Life Support Test Project Phase II test. In order to minimize the fouling of membranes used in the first stage of this system, the fouling of ultrafiltration membranes by surfactants found in personal care products was evaluated. The surfactants and membrane materials were studied to determine if an optimum combination of materials could be found to minimize fouling and maximize contaminant removal. The relationship between micellar properties and ultrafiltration performance were examined to determine if a generic relationship can be developed to predict the suitability of surfactant mixtures for treatment with ultrafiltration. Ultimately, this information will be used to reduce the equivalent system mass of an ultrafiltration/reverse osmosis system. A variety of surfactants and membrane materials were studied to determine if an optimum combination of materials could be found to minimize fouling and maximize contaminant removal. 
<p>
APPROACH:<br>
The methodology for the study isn’t available.
<p>
RESULTS:<br>
This project was cancelled at the start of fiscal year 2004. Results for this study aren’t available.
<p>
